WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.444 --> 00:00:29.118
Thornfield was now a merry place. Every evening the guests gathered in the sitting room to talk or play games while I watched Mr. Rochester and Blanche Ingram from my place on the window seat.

2
00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:38.850
I've told you, reader, that I'd learned to love Mr. Rochester. I could not stop loving him now merely because he'd ceased to notice me.

3
00:00:38.851 --> 00:00:48.574
I was not jealous of Miss Ingram; she was too inferior to excite that feeling. She was very showy, but her mind was poor.

4
00:00:48.575 --> 00:00:54.652
She never had an opinion of her own, and she was often needlessly cruel to Adele.

5
00:00:54.653 --> 00:01:04.663
I realized that Mr. Rochester was going to marry her, perhaps for family or political reasons. But I felt that he did not love her.

6
00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:09.311
What upset me was that she didn't know how to charm him and I did.

7
00:01:09.312 --> 00:01:17.977
If she'd sat quietly by his side, instead of accosting him with brilliant remarks, she might have won his love.

8
00:01:17.978 --> 00:01:23.794
Meanwhile I grew lenient toward my master, forgetting all his faults.

9
00:01:23.795 --> 00:01:32.319
One wet afternoon the guests were restless. Mr. Rochester had ridden out earlier and had not yet returned.

10
00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:38.222
The ladies had proposed visiting a Gypsy camp, but that was postponed due to the weather.

11
00:01:38.223 --> 00:01:43.289
It was almost dusk, and Adele was kneeling by me on the window seat.

12
00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:48.279
"Here's Mr. Rochester!" she cried as a carriage came up the drive.

13
00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:56.446
Blanche Ingram hurried to the window, almost falling over me. With a sneer she moved to another window.

14
00:01:56.447 --> 00:02:01.786
"That's not Mr. Rochester, you tiresome little monkey," she said to Adele.

15
00:02:01.787 --> 00:02:09.778
"Who perched you up there to give false intelligence?" She glanced angrily at me as if I were to blame.

16
00:02:09.779 --> 00:02:18.270
A servant soon brought the stranger to the sitting room. He bowed to Lady Ingram as she was the eldest lady present.

17
00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:24.842
"It seems that I've come at a bad time, madam, when my friend, Mr. Rochester, is away," said the man.

18
00:02:24.843 --> 00:02:34.073
"But I arrive from a very long journey, and I think I may presume on such an old, intimate acquaintance as to remain here until he returns."

19
00:02:34.074 --> 00:02:40.580
He was a handsome man about Mr. Rochester's age, but something about him repelled me.

20
00:02:40.581 --> 00:02:46.555
I soon learned that he was Mr. Mason and that he'd just arrived from Jamaica.

21
00:02:46.556 --> 00:02:57.138
I was wondering how he knew Mr. Rochester when a servant announced that an old Gypsy woman had arrived and wanted to tell the fortunes of the young, single ladies.

22
00:02:57.139 --> 00:03:04.073
She refused to come into the sitting room, insisting that they come to her in the library.

23
00:03:04.074 --> 00:03:08.729
Miss Ingram went first, laughing gaily as she left us.

24
00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:19.209
When she returned, she refused to say what the Gypsy had told her and flounced onto a couch with a book, which she looked at moodily without turning a page.

25
00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:31.365
Her sister, Mary, went next accompanied by the Eshton sisters. They returned shrieking and laughing. "She knows all about us!" they cried.

26
00:03:31.366 --> 00:03:35.679
While I was watching them, a servant appeared at my side.

27
00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:43.624
"If you please, miss, the Gypsy says there's another young, single lady and she refuses to leave until she sees you."

28
00:03:43.625 --> 00:03:48.273
When I entered the library, the Gypsy was sitting by the hearth.

29
00:03:48.274 --> 00:03:55.502
She wore a red cloak and a wide-brimmed, black hat tied under her chin with a striped handkerchief.

30
00:03:55.503 --> 00:04:01.841
The hat brim shaded her face, the lower part of which was concealed by a white scarf.

31
00:04:01.842 --> 00:04:07.673
"Do you want your fortune told?" she asked in a harsh voice.

32
00:04:07.674 --> 00:04:12.321
"I don't care, but I must warn you I don't believe in fortunes."

33
00:04:12.322 --> 00:04:17.000
"You're impudent to say so, but I expected that of you."

34
00:04:17.001 --> 00:04:24.071
I was not impressed with her, but I let her rattle on about my health and other things she thought she knew about me.

35
00:04:24.072 --> 00:04:31.508
When she began to talk of Mr. Rochester, I was still skeptical, but I thought I'd try a question of my own.

36
00:04:31.509 --> 00:04:35.367
"Is Mr. Rochester getting married?" I asked.

37
00:04:35.368 --> 00:04:39.223
"Yes, to the beautiful Blanche Ingram."

38
00:04:39.224 --> 00:04:40.716
"Soon?"

39
00:04:40.717 --> 00:04:52.793
"It appears so. He must love such a handsome, witty, accomplished lady. And she probably loves him or, if not him, at least his fortune."

40
00:04:52.794 --> 00:05:04.614
Here the Gypsy smiled to herself. "I know she considers his fortune very desirable, although I told her something about that which made her frown."

41
00:05:04.615 --> 00:05:10.620
"But I didn't come to hear Mr. Rochester's fortune. I came to hear my own."

42
00:05:10.621 --> 00:05:20.419
The Gypsy beckoned me to come closer, and suddenly I noticed a ring on her finger, a ring that I'd seen a hundred times before.

43
00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:24.279
"Mr. Rochester!" I cried.

44
00:05:24.279 --> 00:05:29.526
He laughed and took off his hat. "Do you forgive me, Jane?"

45
00:05:29.527 --> 00:05:33.179
"I'll try, but it wasn't right to fool the ladies."

46
00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:42.497
"Ah, the ladies." Mr. Rochester removed his cloak as he stood up. "What are they discussing in the sitting room?"

47
00:05:42.498 --> 00:05:48.996
"They're probably talking about the Gypsy, sir. But do you know that a stranger has arrived?"

48
00:05:48.996 --> 00:05:52.484
He shook his head. "Who is it?"

49
00:05:52.485 --> 00:05:57.814
"He comes from the island of Jamaica, sir, and his name is Mason."

50
00:05:57.815 --> 00:06:05.735
"Mason! Jamaica!" Mr. Rochester grasped my wrist convulsively and his smile froze.

51
00:06:05.736 --> 00:06:11.284
"Are you ill, sir?" I was concerned because he'd turned so white.

52
00:06:11.285 --> 00:06:14.809
"I've suffered a blow, Jane." He staggered.

53
00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:17.414
"Oh! Lean on me, sir."

54
00:06:17.415 --> 00:06:27.060
I helped him sit down, and he made me sit beside him. Holding my hand in both of his, he gazed at me in a most distressed way.

55
00:06:27.061 --> 00:06:38.802
"My little friend!" he said. "I wish I were on a quiet island with only you, and that trouble, danger, and all hideous recollections were removed from me."

56
00:06:38.802 --> 00:06:45.046
I was alarmed. "Can I help you? I'd give my life to serve you."

57
00:06:45.047 --> 00:06:51.545
"Thank you, Jane. If all the fine people in the sitting room came here and spat at me, what would you do?"

58
00:06:51.546 --> 00:06:54.204
"Turn them out of the room if I could."

59
00:06:54.205 --> 00:07:03.686
"But what if I tried to talk to them and they only sneered at me and whispered among themselves? What if they abandoned me? Would you go with them?"

60
00:07:03.687 --> 00:07:08.777
"Oh no, sir. I'd rather stay and try to comfort you."

61
00:07:08.778 --> 00:07:12.005
"Could you risk their disapproval for my sake?"

62
00:07:12.006 --> 00:07:17.113
"I'd do it for anyone who deserves my friendship, as I'm sure you do."

63
00:07:17.114 --> 00:07:26.836
He asked me to go into the sitting room and whisper to Mr. Mason that he was wanted in the library by Mr. Rochester. I did as he asked.

64
00:07:26.837 --> 00:07:34.170
Later that night as I was falling asleep, I heard him talking happily as he showed Mason to a bedroom.

65
00:07:34.171 --> 00:07:40.111
Mr. Rochester's cheerful tone set my heart at ease, and I was soon asleep.
